Note digests:
{"note_id":50,"note_type":"Breast Imaging Report","summary":"Screening mammogram identified a developing asymmetry in the left breast, and diagnostic imaging revealed a 2.3 cm irregular hypoechoic mass with suspicious features classified as BI‑RADS 5; left axillary nodes were not enlarged, and an ultrasound‑guided core biopsy was performed on the same day.","flags":["breast imaging","BI-RADS 5","irregular mass","core biopsy","left axillary nodes"]}
{"note_id":51,"note_type":"Pathology Report","summary":"Core needle biopsy of a left breast mass revealed invasive mammary carcinoma with ductal features and concurrent intermediate‑grade DCIS, measuring at least 1.1 cm, estrogen and progesterone receptor positive and HER2 negative, with no lymphovascular invasion. The report confirms malignancy and indicates that definitive tumor size and nodal status will be assessed at excision.","flags":["breast core biopsy","invasive ductal carcinoma","DCIS","ER positive","HER2 negative","pathology report"]}
{"note_id":52,"note_type":"Surgical Oncology Consultation","summary":"The patient was seen after a biopsy confirmed left breast cancer, with MRI demonstrating a solitary lesion and clinical stage cT2 cN0 cM0. A discussion of surgical options concluded with a plan for left lumpectomy and sentinel lymph node biopsy. No evidence of multifocal, contralateral, chest wall invasion, or inflammatory disease was noted.","flags":["left breast cancer","lumpectomy","sentinel lymph node biopsy","MRI","clinical staging","surgical oncology"]}
{"note_id":53,"note_type":"Operative Pathology Report","summary":"The patient underwent a left breast lumpectomy with sentinel lymph node biopsy; pathology showed a 2.4 cm grade 2 invasive ductal carcinoma with intermediate‑grade DCIS, negative margins, and three negative sentinel nodes, staging the disease as pT2 pN0(sn). Post‑operative recovery was uncomplicated aside from mild axillary soreness and a small resolving seroma.","flags":["lumpectomy","invasive ductal carcinoma","sentinel lymph node biopsy","negative margins","stage pT2","seroma"]}
{"note_id":54,"note_type":"Medical Oncology Consultation","summary":"The patient had a medical oncology consultation to discuss adjuvant management after resection of a hormone‑receptor‑positive, HER2‑negative, node‑negative left‑breast invasive cancer larger than 2 cm. A genomic assay was ordered to estimate the benefit of adjuvant chemotherapy, and no staging scans were performed due to the absence of systemic symptoms.","flags":["medical oncology","breast cancer","adjuvant therapy","genomic assay","staging scans"]}
{"note_id":55,"note_type":"Systemic Therapy Note","summary":"An intermediate‑high genomic assay prompted recommendation of adjuvant chemotherapy, and the first docetaxel/cyclophosphamide cycle was administered and tolerated with expected fatigue and metallic taste. A referral for adjuvant whole‑breast radiation after chemotherapy was placed, with endocrine therapy planned thereafter. The patient’s question about the in‑situ component was addressed, emphasizing that the invasive component drives prognosis and treatment planning.","flags":["genomic assay","adjuvant chemotherapy","docetaxel/cyclophosphamide","radiation referral","endocrine therapy","invasive component"]}
{"note_id":56,"note_type":"Clinical History","summary":"The note documents the patient’s past medical history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and a prior benign breast cyst aspiration, with no prior breast malignancy. She reports no nipple discharge, skin ulceration, dyspnea, or neurological symptoms and has an ECOG performance status of 0. Baseline CBC and CMP are adequate for initiating chemotherapy.","flags":["hypertension","hyperlipidemia","benign breast cyst","ECOG 0","chemotherapy labs"]}

